  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Figshare dataset has already shown early scholarly uptake: it has 2 OpenAlex citations and 3 documented downstream reuse signals. The strongest evidence comes from a 2023 preprint and a 2024 Nature Communications article that both cite the dataset in work on beta cell primary cilia, suggesting that the resource is being used in active, high-visibility research on pancreatic function and diabetes.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset provides isotropic 3D electron microscopy reference data for isolated murine pancreatic islets treated with high glucose, supporting structural work at nanometer resolution. Its impact record is still early, but the available evidence points to meaningful scholarly reuse rather than isolated retrieval: OpenAlex records 2 citations, and Crossref data citations show that the dataset was cited as a dataset in both a 2023 preprint and a 2024 journal article.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing literature is especially notable for its venue mix and subject alignment. One downstream use appeared first in bioRxiv and then in Nature Communications, both centered on beta cell primary cilia and pancreatic function. That pattern indicates that the dataset is being taken up in research that is closely related to the original biological system and imaging approach, rather than only being mentioned tangentially.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond formal citations, the reuse graph shows additional documented reuse activity, including an article, a preprint, and a biomedical article mention in Europe PMC. The record does not show social-media or broader public-discourse signals, and usage data remain modest, with 6 recorded views and no downloads in the available DataCite record. Taken together, this is a small but credible early impact profile: limited in volume, but anchored by direct scholarly reuse in relevant, visible research.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Authorship and institutional signals also suggest a resource embedded in a collaborative research network. The dataset is associated with Howard Hughes Medical Institute and Janelia-linked contributors, and the citing works draw on an international, multi-institutional team. In that context, the dataset appears to function as a reusable imaging reference for specialized cell-biological and diabetes-related work, with its main value currently visible in citation-based reuse rather than in public attention.</p>",
